{:id :h1-signed-update-apply
 :date "2026-10-25"
 :row :H1
 :claim "Signed update: the machine takes an update package, verifies its manifest signature and file digests, applies the boot chain, and refuses an unsigned or tampered update."
 :host {:model "MacBookPro18,4", :cpu "Apple M1 Max", :os "Darwin 25.4.0 arm64"}
 :commit "7bcfbd73a0984b8c0af90c5e11bd7245dbc1042d"
 :verdicts {"signed-update-applied" {:green? true, :reason :marker, :marker "AIUEOS_UPDATE_APPLIED manifest-signed copies-verified (run6: RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5-SHA256 manifest verified under the release key, per-file sha256 verified, three files applied to the system ESP with read-back compare)"},
            "unsigned-update-refused" {:green? true, :reason :marker, :marker "AIUEOS_UPDATE_REFUSED_unsigned (run8: a manifest with one flipped byte fails the signature and no file is written)"},
            "system-keeps-booting-after-refusal" {:green? true, :reason :marker, :marker "AIUEOS_DESKTOP_LOOP_OK after the refusal run: the untouched system boots normally"}}
 :run
 {:package "an update volume (FAT16, attached via the harness's new AIUEOS_UPDATE_IMAGE drive) carrying UPDATE.ID, UPDATE.MNF (three <sha256> <name> lines), UPDATE.SIG (openssl dgst -sha256 -sign with qualification/release-key/release-signing-key.pem), and the three boot-chain files at their normal paths"
  :loader "the loader verifies the manifest with a C RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5-SHA256 implementation (the release public key baked as 2048-bit modulus; e=65537 via 16 squarings + one multiply; the reduction algorithm cross-checked against openssl), verifies each file's sha256 against the manifest, then applies through the H3 copy path (READ|WRITE|CREATE+ARCHIVE, read-back compare)"
  :run6 "good package: AIUEOS_UPDATE_APPLIED"
  :run8 "tampered manifest: AIUEOS_UPDATE_REFUSED_unsigned, desktop still boots"}
 :caveats
 ["'the new version runs' is measured as: the applied copies are digest-verified on the system ESP and the system boots to the desktop after applying — the QEMU harness boots with snapshot=on, so the applied bytes live in the run's overlay and a second boot of the same overlay was not captured; a snapshot=off run would close this"
  "'the machine fetches' is the update volume read in the QEMU test; the network fetch is the physical-machine story"
  "the update package carries one build's three files; a version bump between builds is not exercised (the same-build package proves the mechanism)"
  "owner may reject: the manifest format is the three fixed file names (no arbitrary payload sets)"]
 :green? true}
